The '''Lotus Evija''' is a limited production [[Electric car|electric]] sports car manufactured by [[China|Chinese]] owned British automobile manufacturer [[Lotus Cars]]. Unveiled in July 2019, it is the first electric vehicle introduced and manufactured by the company.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://media.lotuscars.com/en/news-articles/evija-lotus-unveils-world%E2%80%99s-most-powerful-production-car.html|title=Lotus unveils world's most powerful production car|date=2019-07-16|website=Lotus Cars}}</ref> Codenamed "Type 130" and "Omega", its production will be limited to 130 units.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wilkinson |first1=Luke |title=Lotus Evija name confirmed for new electric hypercar |url=https://www.autoexpress.co.uk/lotus/107227/lotus-evija-name-confirmed-for-new-electric-hypercar |access-date=5 July 2019 |work=Auto Express |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|last=Duff|first=Mike|date=2019-07-07|title=The Lotus Evija Is a Lean, Mean Electric Hypercar Debuting on July 16|url=https://www.caranddriver.com/news/a27148491/lotus-type-130-electric-hypercar-confirmed/|access-date=2021-10-03|website=Car and Driver|language=en-US}}</ref>

The Evija prototype underwent high-speed testing in November 2019. A video was released on 21 November 2019 ahead of its debut later that day<ref name="CitationB">{{Citation |last=Silvestro |first=Brian |date=November 22, 2019 |title=Watch the 2000-HP Lotus Evija Electric Hypercar on the Move for the First Time |publisher=[[Road & Track]] |url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/future-cars/a29872102/2021-lotus-evija-first-driving-video/ |access-date=November 22, 2019 }}</ref> at the [[Guangzhou Auto Show]].<ref name="CitationA">{{Citation |last=Lawler |first=Richard |date=November 22, 2019 |title=Lotus puts its electric Evija hypercar prototype on the track |publisher=[[engadget]] |url=https://www.engadget.com/2019/11/21/lotus-evija-video/ |access-date=November 22, 2019 }}</ref> Lotus said it was planning thousands of miles of further road testing, on circuits in Europe and on Lotus's own track at Hethel.<ref name="CitationB"/>

The Evija is set at US$2.3 million, before options and taxes.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jones |first=Edward |date=2024-11-06 |title=Lotus Evija Price, Specs & Photos: Learn All About The All-Electric British Hypercar |url=https://news.dupontregistry.com/specs/lotus-evija-price-specs-photos/ |access-date=2024-12-27 |website=duPont REGISTRY News |language=en-US}}</ref>

==Name==
The name ''Evija'' is derived from [[Eve]] of the [[Abrahamic religions]], a name whose etymology can be traced back to the [[Biblical Hebrew]] [[wiktionary:חי|חי]], meaning 'alive', or 'living'.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://jalopnik.com/lotus-evija-ev-hypercar-promises-almost-2000-horsepower-1836307444|title=The Lotus Evija EV Hypercar Promises Almost 2000 Horsepower, Awesome Looks|last=Goy|first=Alex|date=2019-07-16|website=Jalopnik}}</ref> Lotus Cars CEO [[Phil Popham]] said: "Evija is the perfect name for our new car because it is the first all-new car to come from Lotus as part of the wider Geely family. With Geely's support, we are set to create an incredible range of new cars which are true to the Lotus name and DNA."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.netcarshow.com/lotus/2020-evija/|title = Lotus Evija (2020)}}</ref>

==Specifications==
The Evija was initially powered by a {{convert|70|kWh}} battery pack developed in conjunction with [[Williams Grand Prix Engineering|Williams Advanced Engineering]], with electric motors supplied by Integral Powertrain.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.roadandtrack.com/new-cars/future-cars/a28399953/lotus-evija-electric-hypercar-photos-specs-info-price/|title=The Lotus Evija Is Britain's 2000-Horsepower Hyper EV|last=Petrany|first=Mate|date=17 July 2019 |publisher=[[Road & Track]] |access-date=31 May 2020}}</ref> In 2022 Lotus switched to a {{convert|93|kWh}} battery, which [[Unipart]]'s Hyperbat claims to supply.<ref>{{cite press release |author=<!--Not stated--> |title=Hyperbat's EV battery expertise wins £multi-million Lotus Evija contract |url=https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/hyperbats-ev-battery-expertise-wins-multi-million-lotus-evija-contract-301706087.html |location=Oxford, England |publisher=Hyperbat |agency=PR Newswire |date=19 December 2022 |access-date=2024-12-22}}</ref> The four individual motors are placed at the wheels and each is rated at {{Convert|375|kW|PS hp|abbr=on}}, for a combined total output of 1,500&nbsp;kW (2,039 PS; 2,011&nbsp;hp) and {{Convert|1704|Nm|lbft|0|abbr=on}} of torque.<ref name="Calin">{{Cite web|last=Calin|first=Razvan|date=2022-10-14|title=1,500-kW Lotus Evija EV Is the World's Most Powerful Production Car, Hits 217 MPH|url=https://www.autoevolution.com/news/1500-kw-lotus-evija-ev-is-the-world-s-most-powerful-production-car-hits-217-mph-201270.html |website=Auto Evolution}}</ref><ref name="lotusEvijaFittipaldi">{{cite press release |author=<!--Not stated--> |title=History with a future: the Lotus Evija Fittipaldi |url=https://media.lotuscars.com/en/news-articles/history-with-a-future-the-lotus-evija-fittipaldi.html |location=Hethel, UK |publisher=[[Lotus Cars]] |date=October 14, 2022 |access-date=2023-07-11}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Lotus Cars, Evija |url=https://www.lotuscars.com/en-GB/evija |website=Lotus Cars}}</ref> The Evija has magnesium wheels with diameters of {{convert|20|in|disp=flip|abbr=on}} at the front and {{convert|21|in|disp=flip|abbr=on}} at the rear. It uses Pirelli Trofeo R tyres and [[Brembo#Brands|AP Racing]] carbon ceramic disc brakes.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|url=https://newatlas.com/lotus-evija-2000hp-electric-hypercar/60618/|title=2,000-horsepower Lotus Evija becomes the world's most powerful production car|last=Blain|first=Loz|date=2019-07-17|website=New Atlas}}</ref> Lotus claims the Evija will be able to accelerate from 0 to {{Convert|100|kph|mph|abbr=on}} in under 3 seconds, from 0 to {{Convert|300|kph|mph|abbr=on}} in 9.1 seconds, and achieve a limited top speed of {{convert|217|mph|abbr=on|disp=flip}}.<ref name="Calin"/>

== Production ==
Lotus announced in 2020 that production was to begin that summer.<ref name="CitationC">{{cite press release |author=<!--Not stated--> |title=Lotus Evija production comes ALIVE |url=https://media.lotuscars.com/en/news-articles/lotus-evija-production-comes-alive.html |location=Hethel, UK |publisher=[[Lotus Cars]] |date=February 19, 2020 |access-date=February 19, 2020}}</ref> In 2022, it announced "Eight cars now in build, all sold with first customer deliveries early in 2023."<ref name="lotusEvijaFittipaldi"/> The first car was delivered to [[Jenson Button]] in August 2023.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Anderson |first1=Brad |title=Jenson Button Collects His Brawn GP-Inspired Lotus Evija In Monterey |url=https://www.carscoops.com/2023/08/jenson-button-collects-his-brawn-gp-inspired-lotus-evija-in-monterey/ |website=CarScoops |date=21 August 2023 |access-date=13 April 2024}}</ref> In December 2024, Lotus announced again that customer deliveries are underway.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Anderson |first1=Brad |title=Lotus Evija deliveries under way after four-year delay |url=https://www.autocar.co.uk/car-news/new-cars/lotus-evija-deliveries-under-way-after-four-year-delay |publisher=[[Autocar (magazine)|Autocar]] |date=9 December 2024 |access-date=21 December 2024}}</ref>

